    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work as specified.\n\n**API note:** the pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is SDK version 2, which renamed `FastMCP` to `MCPServer`. The old `mcp.server.fastmcp` import now deliberately raises an error. So the server is built with `MCPServer(\"notes\")` and the `@server.tool()` decorator.\n\n- **`add_note`** stores the body in `NOTES[title]` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- **`word_count`** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`. For an unknown title it raises the SDK's `ToolError(\"no such note: <title>\")`. The SDK treats that as an expected failure and sends the message back to the model. Any other exception would show only a generic \"Error executing tool\" message.\n- `python -m app.server` still serves over stdio through `build_server().run()`.\n\n**Checks run:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke` over stdio lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- Direct calls: `add_note` returned `saved a`, and `word_count` returned `4` for `\"one two  three\\nfour\"`.\n- Over stdio, `word_count` on an unknown title returned `is_error=True` with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zz`.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** I couldn't delete my scratch script `_check.py` in the project root because `rm` needs your approval. Please delete it, or tell me to."
